{:edb/db-before-t 1421 :edb/db-after-t 1422 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534734 50 #inst "2026-10-12T09:51:56.243Z" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171477 1078 #edb/ref 17592186171479 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171794 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\n1. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n2. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n3. The weight of a child at birth" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186171794 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n\nII. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n\nIII. The weight of a child at birth" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171794 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171794 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186171795 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/71/714585d89ba3f1d529cd6b64658b778eafa8c346ff9ec592d4997e56803b913e.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda = 1/20$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda = 1/20,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{20} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/9f/9f4bcc34982b48061f0fda3c3136e97f37030cb1cf7d526919860ec53274c644.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-x/20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/20}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/4} \\\\  & ≈0.2212\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171795 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1/20\n$$\n\npurchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1/20\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{20} \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{20}e^{-x/20},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 5) &= \\int_{0}^{5}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}e^{-x/20}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-x/20}\\right|_{0}^{5} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1/4} + e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-1/4} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2212\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186171796 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a82ddd5e5aa9cc641d3720adb9b998c9bc7e72c4e6edfb929d413a6fc584f87.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/79/7960b1171c6697fe49628a8f49ac07671d05acf323ebdfb9734175e12a605640.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 60) & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/30}|}_{60}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186171796 1074 "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1/30\n$$\n\npurchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1/30\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{30} \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{30}e^{-x/30},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 60) &= \\int_{60}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{60}^{∞}\\frac{1}{30}e^{-x/30}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-x/30}\\right|_{60}^{∞} \\\\\n&=-e^{- ∞} + e^{-2} \\\\\n&= 0 + e^{-2} \\\\\n&= e^{-2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1353\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186171797 1074 "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$\n\nrequests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{30} \\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t$, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$\n\n$s=30$, and $t=20$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 30 + 20 \\mid X > 30) &= P(X > 20) \\\\\n&= e^{- λt} \\\\\n&= e^{- (1/30) \\cdot (20)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2/3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.513\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186171797 1074 "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}$ requests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/30)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.513.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434466 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434467 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434468 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172175 1050 900.0 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172642 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\n1. The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads\n2. The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads\n3. The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads" 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186172642 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads\n\nII. The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads\n\nIII. The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186172642 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Incidents can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next incident occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of incidents in a month is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186173121 1064 "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda.$ \n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/72/72cd99ec8ff51659357f00417203ace84164027e0cb88584cc136f608d86b15a.svg)\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-3x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]+[1] \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.9502.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186173121 1064 "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda$.\n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1) &= \\int_{0}^{1}3e^{-3x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-3x}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [- e^{-3}] - [- e^{0}] \\\\\n&= [- e^{-3}] + [1] \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9502\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186173122 1064 "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed.\n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{decays/minute}\n$$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 1) &= \\int_{0}^{1}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}0.8e^{-0.8x}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-0.8x}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.8} + e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-0.8} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1$, so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." 13194139534734 17592186045417 false] [17592186173122 1064 "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed. \n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{ decays/minute}.$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-0.8x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.5507.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1,$ so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big|\\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434448 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434448 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434