17592186434466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434466 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time until a laser fails, in hours, in a specific type of machine\n\nII. The number obtained when launching a fair roulette wheel\n\nIII. The number of children in a daycare center" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434466 1072 #uuid "3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434466 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Laser failure in a particular machine can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the time it takes for the laser to fail.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. Moreover, the number obtained when launching a roulette wheel is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of children is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434463 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1069 "q-96681" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time it takes for a taxi to reach any address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nII. The time it takes for a taxi to reach a particular address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nIII. The number of defective electronic components produced in a factory every week" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1072 #uuid "5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434467 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow an exponential distribution. The times needed to reach different destinations will likely have different average rates.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Journeys along a particular route can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time needed to reach the destination.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective components is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434465 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1069 "q-132463" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time between customer calls, in minutes, for a particular business\n\nII. The number of winning lottery tickets in a set of $10$ tickets\n\nIII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $0$ and $15$ at random" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1072 #uuid "c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true] [17592186434468 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled using an exponential distribution. Calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next call occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of winning lottery tickets is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number obtained is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534734 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00635c76-6a2d-5359-9c79-cf68e4a537ae" 17592186434448 "answer-112ab786-9dc2-56b1-ad95-98851cd1d5fc" 17592186434449 "answer-154c2087-4f7e-5899-993c-d47ac1fb33e2" 17592186434450 "answer-1916b91d-3dd2-5b7d-8065-6c9e62cb7944" 17592186434451 "answer-2923f92c-f047-52fe-9354-4e133888c105" 17592186434452 "answer-48c12e76-6dfc-5fcc-8a40-a983537294e7" 17592186434453 "answer-4a18a4a3-0167-535b-9b6b-71a22c559836" 17592186434454 "answer-4c961182-ebbc-558a-a08d-aab935e1f068" 17592186434455 "answer-67e34ad7-4389-5131-b10c-f7c33576ead6" 17592186434456 "answer-8cfc39d2-61a4-56e6-90bf-ad85f0cc4e4c" 17592186434457 "answer-9866233c-de4a-59cc-a272-02e41e15cca9" 17592186434458 "answer-a468f832-3368-5771-bc32-4531a9f82886" 17592186434459 "answer-bf065d18-e87f-5a12-9d23-987422ac4b16" 17592186434460 "ans